        "explanation": "This sentence contains four redundancy problems: (1) \"ancient... which are old\" — \"ancient\" means old; the relative clause restates the adjective; (2) \"decaying for centuries\" is partially implied by \"ancient ruins\" — ancient ruins are, by nature, in decay; (3) \"a reminder that reminds us\" — \"reminder\" already means something that reminds; the relative clause is a pure tautology; (4) \"origins and beginnings\" — exact synonyms; keep one. Option B eliminates all four: \"ancient\" (drops \"which are old\"), no decay clause (implied), \"a reminder\" (drops \"that reminds us\"), and \"origins\" (drops \"beginnings\"). Option C removes the \"which are old\" clause but keeps \"today\" (redundant with \"still stand\" — standing implies the present) and \"earliest origins\" (\"origins\" are by definition the earliest point). Option D drops the redundant relative clause but uses \"earliest beginnings\" — \"earliest\" is redundant because \"beginnings\" already refers to the start. Option B is the only choice that clears all four problems.",
        "success_narrative": "You hand back the corrected sentence. The Steward reads it, and his elaborate sentence — stripped of all its padding — stands clean and precise. He steps aside. The inner palace is before you.",
        "failure_narrative": "You choose option C. The Steward circles \"today\" with satisfaction. \"Necessary emphasis,\" he says. You step aside and let him win this one.",
