        "correct_answer": "B",
        "explanation": "The passage is an official royal proclamation — a formal legal document. Its register is established by phrases like \"shall commence,\" \"are required to,\" and \"appropriate legal consequences.\" These are formal, impersonal, and authoritative. Option A (the original) is casual and conversational: \"super important,\" \"fill this out right away,\" and \"don't have any issues\" all belong to informal speech. Option C (\"really should,\" \"honestly\") and option D (\"pretty urgent,\" \"like, now\") are similarly informal and introduce colloquial filler words. Option B, \"Prompt and accurate completion of the registration is essential to avoid administrative penalties,\" matches the existing register precisely: it is impersonal, formal, and uses vocabulary consistent with official legal language (\"prompt,\" \"essential,\" \"administrative penalties\"). On the ACT, register-matching means identifying the level of formality in the passage and choosing the option that fits — neither too casual nor anachronistically stiff compared to the surrounding text.",
