        "explanation": "The passage establishes a formal, analytical register through phrases like \"increased markedly,\" \"owing primarily to,\" \"advances in... technology,\" and \"demonstrated exceptional yields.\" The underlined sentence breaks this register sharply. Option A uses slang (\"totally crushing it\"), filler (\"like\"), and informal comparison (\"way better than everyone expected\"). Option B (\"done a really solid job\") improves but retains informal qualifiers: \"frankly\" and \"solid job\" are conversational. Option D (\"quite a few people, to say the least\") uses informal hedging and an idiomatic phrase that belongs in speech, not policy writing. Option C, \"Northern agricultural output has consistently surpassed projected benchmarks,\" matches the passage's register exactly: it is impersonal, analytical, uses precise vocabulary (\"surpassed,\" \"benchmarks\"), and parallels the phrasing of the surrounding sentences. Notice the ACT tests middle-register options (option B) as traps — they seem better than the obvious casual choice but still contain informal elements.",
