        "explanation": "The passage is a practical safety notice written in plain, direct language: \"leave the building immediately,\" \"do not use the service elevators.\" The register is clear and accessible — formal enough to be official, but not legalistic. Option A uses bureaucratic language (\"pursuant to,\" \"aforementioned protocols of egress\") that is far more formal than the surrounding text and would confuse the intended audience. Option C is too casual: \"Everyone should head to\" and \"which is the official place to go\" are informal and vague. Option B, \"The meeting point is the east courtyard,\" is almost right but \"meeting point\" is more casual than the passage's register warrants — the passage uses clear but slightly formal vocabulary (\"immediately,\" \"designated\"). Option D, \"The designated assembly point is the east courtyard,\" matches precisely: \"designated\" is direct and official, \"assembly point\" is the standard term for emergency contexts, and the sentence structure mirrors the direct imperative style of the surrounding text. When the ACT passage is accessible but professional, avoid both bureaucratic overreach and casual underperformance.",
