        "enemy_name": "The Mismatched Minister",
        "enemy_type": "Palace Courtier",
        "pre_narrative": "The Mismatched Minister writes the Keep's diplomatic correspondence. His letters to foreign rulers begin formally, then slip into casual observations, then recover, then slip again. The northern Duke has written back asking if the Keep's royal scribe is \"feeling alright.\" You must stabilize the next letter.",
        "passage": "The Kingdom of the Keep extends its sincere greetings to the Duchy of Ironhold and reaffirms its commitment to the longstanding alliance between our two realms. We write to address the matter of the disputed border territories in the Thornwood region. [1] These lands have historically belonged to the Keep, based on the Treaty of the Ashen Gate signed in the third year of King Aldric's reign. [2] And honestly, we think the Duke probably knows this already and just wants to stir up trouble, but whatever.",
        "question_stem": "Which choice best replaces sentence [2] to maintain the formal diplomatic register established by sentences [1] and earlier?",
        "option_a": "And honestly, we think the Duke probably knows this already and just wants to stir up trouble, but whatever.",
        "option_b": "The Keep therefore asserts its lawful claim and requests that the Duchy acknowledge the historical precedent established by the treaty.",
        "option_c": "We're pretty sure the Duke knows this, so it's a bit odd that this is even a conversation we're having.",
        "option_d": "The Duke is clearly aware of this, and frankly his position is hard to defend from a historical standpoint.",
        "correct_answer": "B",
        "explanation": "The passage establishes a formal diplomatic register in every sentence: \"extends its sincere greetings,\" \"reaffirms its commitment,\" \"longstanding alliance,\" \"historically belonged,\" \"Treaty of the Ashen Gate.\" This is the language of international statecraft. Option A crashes this register completely with \"honestly,\" \"stir up trouble,\" and \"whatever\" — casual, dismissive, and inappropriate for diplomatic correspondence. Option C (\"pretty sure,\" \"a bit odd,\" \"a conversation we're having\") is similarly informal. Option D is better — \"clearly aware\" and \"hard to defend\" are not slang — but \"frankly\" is a conversational hedging word that breaks the formal register, and \"hard to defend from a historical standpoint\" is slightly informal in phrasing. Option B, \"The Keep therefore asserts its lawful claim and requests that the Duchy acknowledge the historical precedent established by the treaty,\" is precisely calibrated: it is formal, assertive without being belligerent, uses precise legal-diplomatic language (\"asserts its lawful claim,\" \"historical precedent\"), and follows naturally from sentence [1]. Option D is the hardest wrong answer here — the ACT tests whether students can spot \"almost formal but not quite.\"",
        "success_narrative": "The letter is delivered to the Duke of Ironhold. He reads it, confers with his advisors, and agrees to a formal review of the border claim. Diplomacy succeeds.",
        "failure_narrative": "You choose option D. The Duke's advisors note the word \"frankly\" and file it as evidence of weakness in the Keep's position. Negotiations stall.",
