        "enemy_name": "The Tonal Traitor",
        "enemy_type": "Palace Courtier",
        "pre_narrative": "The Tonal Traitor writes scholarship essays for the Keep's academy. He has a PhD in history but learned to write in a tavern. His latest essay on the decline of the Second Keep begins with three paragraphs of excellent scholarship, then suddenly shifts to a passage that sounds like a personal diary entry. You must fix the shift.",
        "passage": "Historians have long debated the precise causes of the Second Keep's dissolution in the fourth century. Most scholarly accounts emphasize the role of prolonged drought, weakening central authority, and persistent conflict with neighboring territories. Recent archaeological evidence, however, suggests that internal economic collapse may have preceded these better-known factors. [1] Excavations at the ruins of the Old Market Quarter have revealed a dramatic decline in trade goods beginning approximately forty years before the traditional date assigned to the Keep's fall. [2] It's kind of wild, when you think about it — like, the whole thing was basically falling apart from the inside way before anyone noticed.",
        "question_stem": "Which choice best replaces sentence [2] to maintain the formal, scholarly tone of the passage?",
        "option_a": "It's kind of wild, when you think about it — like, the whole thing was basically falling apart from the inside way before anyone noticed.",
        "option_b": "This finding is significant, as it implies the Second Keep's collapse was well underway internally before external pressures became apparent.",
        "option_c": "This discovery is pretty surprising and changes how we think about the whole collapse.",
        "option_d": "In other words, the collapse was an inside job, so to speak, that historians missed for a long time.",
