        "explanation": "The passage is a formal academic essay. Markers include: \"Historians have long debated,\" \"scholarly accounts emphasize,\" \"archaeological evidence suggests,\" \"internal economic collapse may have preceded.\" This register is analytical, impersonal, and precise. Option A breaks the register entirely: \"It's kind of wild,\" \"like,\" \"the whole thing was basically\" — all casual filler. Option C (\"pretty surprising,\" \"changes how we think\") is casual and vague — \"pretty surprising\" is too informal and \"changes how we think about the whole collapse\" is imprecise. Option D (\"an inside job, so to speak\") uses a colloquial idiom and the hedging phrase \"so to speak,\" both of which are inappropriate in academic writing. Option B, \"This finding is significant, as it implies the Second Keep's collapse was well underway internally before external pressures became apparent,\" matches the register: \"This finding is significant\" echoes scholarly acknowledgment language; \"implies\" and \"external pressures\" are appropriately precise; and the causal logic is stated clearly without colloquialisms. The ACT lesson: in scholarly or professional passages, watch for opinion language (\"kind of,\" \"basically,\" \"pretty\"), contractions in formal contexts, and idioms that belong in speech.",
