        "option_a": "tbh the sheer scale of it was insane and it's honestly impressive anyone pulled it off.",
        "option_b": "The magnitude of this undertaking, by any measure, stands as a remarkable achievement of early Keep civilization.",
        "option_c": "It was, in a word, a massive achievement that really shows what the early Keep was capable of.",
        "option_d": "This was, without a doubt, one of the most impressive construction projects the region had ever seen.",
        "correct_answer": "B",
        "explanation": "The passage is formal historical prose, established by diction like \"pivotal moment,\" \"unprecedented scale,\" \"precise interlocking stonework,\" and \"renowned throughout the known world.\" Option A is completely incongruous: \"tbh\" (text abbreviation), \"insane\" (slang), and \"pulled it off\" (idiomatic) all belong to informal digital communication, not historical writing. Option C (\"massive achievement,\" \"really shows what\") is informal — \"really shows\" is conversational and \"massive\" is colloquial in this context. Option D is closer but still uses \"without a doubt\" (a common speech phrase) and \"most impressive... the region had ever seen\" — functional but somewhat vague and informal compared to the passage's precise vocabulary. Option B, \"The magnitude of this undertaking, by any measure, stands as a remarkable achievement of early Keep civilization,\" uses elevated, precise vocabulary (\"magnitude,\" \"undertaking,\" \"remarkable achievement\") that directly mirrors the register of the surrounding sentences. \"By any measure\" is slightly rhetorical but appropriate for formal historical writing. Note how \"remarkable achievement of early Keep civilization\" parallels \"renowned throughout the known world\" from sentence [1].",
