        "enemy_name": "The Erratic Essayist",
        "enemy_type": "Palace Courtier",
        "pre_narrative": "The Erratic Essayist has written a personal essay about his experience crossing the Thornwood in winter. Personal essays are allowed to be informal and reflective — but this one swings from warm personal storytelling to suddenly dry, technical passages and back again. You must identify and fix the section that breaks the personal, reflective tone.",
        "passage": "The morning I left for the Thornwood crossing, frost covered every stone in the courtyard. I pulled my cloak tighter and reminded myself that I had survived worse winters — though in that moment, I was not sure I believed it. The path through the Thornwood winds for approximately eleven miles, gaining eight hundred feet of elevation before descending sharply toward the valley. [1] It smells of pine and cold stone, and every creak of a branch makes your heart do something embarrassing. [2] Average annual snowfall in the Thornwood region is 74 inches, with peak accumulation typically occurring in the third and fourth months of winter.",
        "question_stem": "Which choice best replaces sentence [2] to maintain the personal, reflective tone established throughout the passage?",
        "option_a": "Average annual snowfall in the Thornwood region is 74 inches, with peak accumulation typically occurring in the third and fourth months of winter.",
        "option_b": "The snow that day was deep enough to swallow a boot, and I found myself wondering who had last walked this path before me.",
        "option_c": "Statistically speaking, the Thornwood receives more snowfall than any other region of the Keep.",
        "option_d": "Snow is common in the Thornwood, and travelers are advised to prepare accordingly.",
        "correct_answer": "B",
        "explanation": "The passage is a first-person personal narrative established through voice markers: \"The morning I left,\" \"I pulled my cloak tighter,\" \"I was not sure I believed it,\" \"your heart do something embarrassing.\" This is intimate, reflective, and sensory. Option A (the original) is a jarring tonal break: \"Average annual snowfall... 74 inches... peak accumulation\" is dry statistical language from a geography report, not a personal essay. Option C (\"Statistically speaking\") is even more clinical. Option D (\"travelers are advised to prepare accordingly\") shifts to instructional register — appropriate for a travel guide but not a personal narrative. Option B, \"The snow that day was deep enough to swallow a boot, and I found myself wondering who had last walked this path before me,\" maintains the first-person voice (\"I found myself\"), uses sensory detail (\"deep enough to swallow a boot\"), and adds the reflective quality that defines the passage's tone (\"wondering who had last walked this path\"). On the ACT, personal narratives and memoir-style passages require options that maintain the subjective, sensory, first-person voice — not statistics, travel advice, or external facts.",
