        "explanation": "The passage is a formal, evidence-based editorial. Its persuasive register relies on data (\"fifteen to twenty percent\"), objective framing (\"economic assessments have projected,\" \"comparable port expansions\"), and measured tone. Option A abandons this completely: \"Honestly,\" \"ignorant of the facts,\" \"actively working against,\" and \"get out of the way\" are aggressive, personal attacks that replace evidence-based argument with hostility. Option D (\"frankly baffling\") retains frustration and informality. Option C, \"Opponents of the expansion would do well to reconsider their position in light of this compelling data,\" is closer — it is formal and data-referencing — but \"would do well to\" is a slightly condescending construction that edges toward the dismissiveness of option A. Option B, \"Given the strength of this evidence, opposition to the expansion appears difficult to justify on economic grounds,\" is the most precisely calibrated: it is formal, references the evidence already cited, and frames the opposing position critically without personal attack or condescension. The phrase \"appears difficult to justify on economic grounds\" is measured persuasion — it allows for other possible grounds of opposition while firmly asserting the economic case.",
        "success_narrative": "The editorial is published. Several council members who had opposed the expansion publicly acknowledge the strength of the economic argument. The harbor expansion is approved at the next session.",
