        "enemy_type": "Palace Courtier",
        "pre_narrative": "The Wavering Warden of Words has written a solemn memorial address for the fallen soldiers of the Keep's last campaign. It is almost perfect. But in the middle of a sentence about sacrifice and honor, he has inserted a phrase so casual that three mourners left the ceremony early. You must restore the solemnity.",
        "passage": "We gather today to honor those who gave their lives in defense of the Keep. Their sacrifice was not made in pursuit of glory or reward, but out of a profound sense of duty to the people and values they swore to protect. History will record their names among those who stood when others could not. [1] Let the stones of this memorial speak across the centuries to all who come after — that here stood men and women who faced the darkness without turning away. [2] Basically, they were awesome people and we should all be grateful they had our backs.",
        "question_stem": "Which choice best replaces sentence [2] to maintain the solemn, elevated tone of the memorial address?",
        "option_a": "Basically, they were awesome people and we should all be grateful they had our backs.",
        "option_b": "May their courage inspire in us a commitment worthy of the lives they gave.",
        "option_c": "We are thankful for their courage and will never forget what they did for us.",
        "option_d": "They were brave, and the Keep owes them a debt that can never truly be repaid.",
        "correct_answer": "B",
        "explanation": "The passage is an elevated, ceremonial address. Its register is marked by formal, solemn language: \"profound sense of duty,\" \"History will record,\" \"Let the stones of this memorial speak across the centuries,\" \"faced the darkness without turning away.\" This is deliberate, rhetorical prose aimed at solemnity. Option A destroys the tone entirely: \"Basically,\" \"awesome people,\" and \"had our backs\" are casual slang that would be jarring at a funeral, let alone a formal memorial address. Option C (\"We are thankful,\" \"will never forget what they did for us\") is sincere but informal in phrasing — \"what they did for us\" sounds conversational next to \"a profound sense of duty.\" Option D (\"brave,\" \"owes them a debt that can never truly be repaid\") is adequate but lacks the elevated rhetorical quality of the surrounding text — \"brave\" is plain where the passage uses \"courage,\" and \"truly be repaid\" is slightly informal. Option B, \"May their courage inspire in us a commitment worthy of the lives they gave,\" matches the passage's register precisely: \"May their courage inspire in us\" is a formal optative construction (a wish or exhortation) common in ceremonial language; \"a commitment worthy of the lives they gave\" echoes the theme of sacrifice. It elevates rather than deflates the close of the address.",
        "success_narrative": "The memorial address is delivered in full. There are no early departures. Several soldiers in attendance are seen wiping their eyes. The Warden of Words has, for once, said nothing more than was needed.",
        "failure_narrative": "You choose option D. It is acceptable. But a Keep poet in the audience mutters that the ending \"lets the air out\" of the speech.",
