        "correct_answer": "B",
        "explanation": "The sentence evaluates the performance of sealed chambers in a practical, functional context: preventing moisture damage. The word needed must describe functional success — something that works to achieve its purpose. \"Good\" (option A) is a vague general-purpose adjective that provides no specific information about type or degree of success. \"Nice\" (option C) carries a pleasant, social connotation — we describe people and aesthetics as \"nice,\" not machinery and storage systems; it is imprecise here. \"Satisfying\" (option D) describes something that fulfills an expectation or desire — it is primarily subjective and emotional; storage chambers don't satisfy in any meaningful technical sense. \"Effective\" (option B) means producing the intended result — exactly what \"preventing moisture damage\" requires. It is both precise (functional success) and appropriate for the formal, evaluative register of the passage. The ACT's principle on word precision: in professional or technical contexts, avoid vague evaluative adjectives (\"good,\" \"nice,\" \"great\") when a word with specific meaning (\"effective,\" \"efficient,\" \"reliable\") is available.",
        "success_narrative": "The appraiser adds \"effective\" to his vocabulary and begins using it in all his reports. The insurance office finally receives documents they can act on.",
