        "explanation": "The sentence evaluates combat drills in terms of how well they prepared soldiers for a specific operational scenario (nighttime patrol). The word needed must describe functional success — drills that achieved their training goal. \"Okay\" (option A) is vague, informal, and evaluatively empty in a formal report context. \"Pleasant\" (option C) describes an emotional or sensory quality — drills might be unpleasant by design and still be excellent training; the word doesn't measure success. \"Likable\" (option D) applies to people and personalities, not training programs; it is both imprecise and informal. \"Successful\" (option B) directly and precisely states that the drills achieved their purpose. In the context of a formal inspection report that evaluates training outcomes, \"successful\" is the only option that meaningfully communicates achievement of a goal. Note: \"effective\" could also work, but it is not among the options.",
