        "enemy_type": "Palace Courtier",
        "pre_narrative": "The Final Style Sentinel stands at the gate to the inner sanctum of the Style Citadel. He holds a scroll — the Keep's official account of its legal history. One word in the scroll is wrong. Not grammatically wrong. Not redundant. Just imprecise — the wrong word for the context. You must find it and replace it before he lets you pass.",
        "passage": "The principle of habeas corpus was established in the Keep's legal code during the reign of Queen Mira the Just. This doctrine requires that individuals detained by royal authority be brought before a judicial officer who must assess whether their imprisonment is legally justified. The adoption of this principle represented a strong limitation on the unchecked power of the crown.",
        "question_stem": "Which word most precisely replaces the underlined word \"strong\" in the passage?",
        "option_a": "strong",
        "option_b": "significant",
        "option_c": "notable",
        "option_d": "fundamental",
        "correct_answer": "D",
        "explanation": "The sentence describes what the adoption of habeas corpus represented in the context of the crown's power. The word modifying \"limitation\" needs to capture not just the degree of the limitation but its nature and importance within a legal framework. \"Strong\" (option A) suggests physical force or intensity — appropriate for physical objects or emotional statements, but imprecise for describing the nature of a legal doctrine. \"Notable\" (option C) means worthy of attention — it describes how remarkable something is, not how structurally important it is. \"Significant\" (option B) indicates large degree of importance, which is accurate, but remains somewhat vague about the kind of importance. \"Fundamental\" (option D) means forming a necessary base or core element — it captures the idea that habeas corpus was not merely a large or notable limitation on power, but a foundational one that changed the structure of the relationship between the crown and its subjects. In legal and philosophical contexts, \"fundamental\" precisely denotes a structural or constitutional significance that \"strong,\" \"significant,\" and \"notable\" only approximate. The ACT tests these near-synonym distinctions in formal passages: the right word is not merely positive or large-sounding but accurately matches the conceptual register of the surrounding argument.",
