        "pre_narrative": "The King is dictating a decree that uses \"not only... but also\" seven times. Most usages are wrong. You catch the worst one.",
        "passage": null,
        "question_stem": "Which version of the following sentence uses parallel structure correctly with correlative conjunctions?\n",
        "option_a": "The new garrison policy not only reduces costs but also the efficiency of the watch is improved.",
        "option_b": "The new garrison policy not only reduces costs but also improves the efficiency of the watch.",
        "option_c": "The new garrison policy reduces not only costs but also the watch's efficiency is improved.",
        "option_d": "Not only does the new garrison policy cost less, but the efficiency is also improved by it.",
        "correct_answer": "B",
        "explanation": "Correlative conjunctions — \"not only... but also,\" \"either... or,\" \"both... and,\" \"neither... nor\" — require that the grammatical elements following each conjunction be parallel in structure. \"Not only X but also Y\" requires X and Y to be the same part of speech or phrase type. Option A: \"not only reduces costs\" (verb phrase) but \"but also the efficiency of the watch is improved\" (noun clause) — not parallel; the second element is a clause, not a verb phrase. Option C: \"reduces not only costs\" (noun) \"but also the watch's efficiency is improved\" (clause) — not parallel. Option D: \"not only does the new garrison policy cost less\" (full clause) \"but the efficiency is also improved by it\" (full clause) — this structure is grammatically parallel at the clause level, but \"also\" is misplaced (it should follow \"but,\" not sit mid-clause) and the passive voice is awkward. Option B: \"not only reduces costs\" (verb + object) \"but also improves the efficiency of the watch\" (verb + object) — perfectly parallel. Both elements are verb phrases with the same subject (\"the new garrison policy\"). The key rule: identify the structure after the first correlative and exactly mirror it after the second.",
        "success_narrative": "\"Not only correct but also elegant,\" you tell the King. He considers crossing out \"elegant.\" He does not.",
