        "pre_narrative": "The King beams and slides a sentence across the table. \"This,\" he says, \"is my masterpiece of language.\" The sentence says one thing in six different ways. You must find the one that says it correctly once.",
        "passage": null,
        "question_stem": "Which version of the following sentence is most concise while retaining full meaning?\n\nThe new innovation introduced by the Keep's engineers was unprecedented in that nothing like it had ever been seen or attempted before in prior history.",
        "option_a": "The new innovation introduced by the Keep's engineers was unprecedented in that nothing like it had ever been seen or attempted before in prior history.",
        "option_b": "The innovation introduced by the Keep's engineers was unprecedented.",
        "option_c": "The new innovation by the Keep's engineers had never been seen before.",
        "option_d": "The Keep's engineers introduced an innovation that was entirely new and had no prior precedent.",
        "correct_answer": "B",
        "explanation": "This sentence contains multiple redundancies: (1) \"new innovation\" — an innovation is by definition new; \"new\" is redundant; (2) \"unprecedented in that nothing like it had ever been seen or attempted before\" — \"unprecedented\" means never having occurred before; the entire \"in that\" clause explains what \"unprecedented\" already states; (3) \"before in prior history\" — \"before\" and \"prior\" and \"history\" all refer to the past; triple redundancy in three words. Option A keeps every redundancy. Option C removes the clause but keeps \"new\" before \"innovation\" and \"before\" at the end — two remaining redundancies. Option D (\"entirely new and had no prior precedent\") creates two new redundancies: \"entirely new\" is redundant with \"innovation,\" and \"no prior precedent\" is redundant because \"precedent\" by definition refers to what came before. Option B, \"The innovation introduced by the Keep's engineers was unprecedented,\" removes all redundancies: \"innovation\" (not \"new innovation\"), \"unprecedented\" (not explained by a clause), and nothing about \"before\" or \"history.\" Clean and complete.",
        "success_narrative": "The King stares at option B. Every layer of his beloved redundancy has been stripped away. \"But... where did the words go?\" he asks. \"They were never needed,\" you reply.",
        "failure_narrative": "You choose option C. \"New\" before \"innovation\" remains. The King saves it as a partial victory.",
