        "enemy_name": "The Redundancy King",
        "enemy_type": "Final Boss",
        "pre_narrative": "The King's latest decree has commas where they don't belong and none where they do. He says commas are decorative. You say they are functional. This is the debate the palace has been having for a century.",
        "passage": null,
        "question_stem": "Which sentence uses comma placement correctly?\n",
        "option_a": "After the last treaty was signed the palace guard stood down from high alert.",
        "option_b": "After the last treaty was signed, the palace guard stood down from high alert.",
        "option_c": "After the last treaty, was signed the palace guard stood down from high alert.",
        "option_d": "After the last treaty was signed the palace, guard stood down from high alert.",
        "correct_answer": "B",
        "explanation": "The rule for introductory clauses: when a dependent clause or long introductory phrase precedes the main clause, it is followed by a comma. \"After the last treaty was signed\" is an introductory adverbial clause — it tells us when the main action (standing down) occurred. It precedes the main clause \"the palace guard stood down from high alert.\" A comma is required after the introductory clause. Option A omits the comma — this is an error, though a mild one that can create momentary ambiguity. Option B places the comma correctly after the introductory clause, before the main clause begins. Option C inserts a comma after \"treaty,\" splitting the introductory clause in the middle: \"After the last treaty\" is not a complete introductory unit — the clause isn't finished until \"signed.\" This misplacement separates \"treaty\" from \"was signed,\" its own verb. Option D places the comma after \"palace,\" separating the subject \"palace guard\" from itself — it treats \"palace\" as a separate element, which is wrong. The guiding principle: commas after introductory clauses mark the transition point to the main clause; they go at the boundary, not inside either element.",
        "success_narrative": "The decree is posted with correct comma placement. The palace logician nods approvingly. The King adds three more commas decoratively, but none to this sentence.",
        "failure_narrative": "The King sees no need for a comma in option A. \"The reader will figure it out,\" he says.",
