        "enemy_name": "The Redundancy King",
        "enemy_type": "Final Boss",
        "pre_narrative": "The King hands you a passage from the palace's new civic history. \"The transitions,\" he says, \"are my personal choices.\" Every transition is wrong. You must fix the worst one.",
        "passage": "The Keep's early judicial system was informal and inconsistent. Disputes were resolved by local lords, whose decisions varied widely and often reflected personal interests rather than established principles. [A] Furthermore, the crown eventually recognized that a unified legal code was necessary to ensure consistent treatment of all citizens. This recognition led directly to the drafting of the Founding Statutes.",
        "question_stem": "The transition word \"Furthermore\" at the start of sentence [A] is underlined. Which transition word or phrase best fits the logical relationship between sentences?",
        "option_a": "Furthermore,",
        "option_b": "However,",
        "option_c": "Similarly,",
        "option_d": "In other words,",
        "correct_answer": "B",
        "explanation": "Transition words signal logical relationships between ideas. The question is: what is the relationship between sentence 1 (the early system was inconsistent and biased) and sentence [A] (the crown recognized the need for a unified code)? The early system was broken → the crown responded by moving toward a unified code. This is a contrast/pivot: from problem to solution, from disorder to reform effort. \"Furthermore\" (option A) signals addition — it would mean the second idea continues or extends the first in the same direction. But the crown's response is not more of the same problem; it is a reaction against it. \"Similarly\" (option C) signals comparison — the crown's response is not similar to the earlier disorder. \"In other words\" (option D) signals restatement — but the crown's recognition of needing reform is not a restatement of the biased local system; it's a new development. \"However\" (option B) signals contrast or pivot — it correctly marks the shift from describing the broken system to describing the corrective response. Transition questions on the ACT require identifying the direction of the logical relationship (addition, contrast, cause-effect, example, restatement) and matching the transition word to it.",
        "success_narrative": "\"However,\" you say. The King frowns. \"But I wanted furthermore.\" \"The logic does not support it,\" you explain. He writes it down anyway, then crosses it out.",
        "failure_narrative": "\"Furthermore!\" says the King. The passage now implies the reform was an additional problem, not a solution.",
