        "enemy_type": "Final Boss",
        "pre_narrative": "The King rises from his throne. \"Enough warmup,\" he declares. \"The real battles begin.\" He drops a scroll on the floor and waits. The questions get harder from here.",
        "passage": null,
        "question_stem": "Which sentence uses commas correctly with all its elements?\n",
        "option_a": "The historian, a renowned scholar of the Fourth Era delivered her lecture, her voice steady despite the crowd's noise.",
        "option_b": "The historian a renowned scholar of the Fourth Era, delivered her lecture, her voice steady despite the crowd's noise.",
        "option_c": "The historian, a renowned scholar of the Fourth Era, delivered her lecture, her voice steady despite the crowd's noise.",
        "option_d": "The historian, a renowned scholar of the Fourth Era, delivered her lecture her voice steady, despite the crowd's noise.",
        "correct_answer": "C",
        "explanation": "This sentence contains two separate non-essential elements that each require comma pairs: (1) \"a renowned scholar of the Fourth Era\" — an appositive renaming \"the historian,\" which is nonrestrictive and must be set off by two commas; (2) \"her voice steady despite the crowd's noise\" — an absolute phrase (noun + adjective/participle) that modifies the entire main clause and must be set off by a comma. Option A: the second comma of the appositive is missing — \"a renowned scholar of the Fourth Era delivered\" runs on without a closing comma around the appositive. Option B: the opening comma of the appositive is missing — \"The historian a renowned scholar\" runs on. Option D: the comma after \"lecture\" is missing — the absolute phrase \"her voice steady despite the crowd's noise\" is not set off from the main clause. Option C correctly places commas both around the appositive (after \"historian\" and after \"Era\") and before the absolute phrase (after \"lecture\"). The rule: both appositives and absolute phrases are nonrestrictive elements requiring comma pairs (or a single comma when at the end of a sentence).",
        "success_narrative": "The King examines every comma in option C. He can find no fault. His own prose has no absolute phrases because he does not trust them.",
        "failure_narrative": "You choose option A. The King spots the missing comma and celebrates. One small victory for redundancy.",
