        "enemy_name": "The Redundancy King",
        "enemy_type": "Final Boss",
        "pre_narrative": "\"Semicolons,\" the King announces. \"A coward's punctuation — neither a comma nor a period.\" He is wrong about this, as he is wrong about most things. You will demonstrate the correct use.",
        "passage": null,
        "question_stem": "Which version of the following sentence is punctuated correctly?\n",
        "option_a": "The eastern trade route had been closed for three seasons, therefore, merchants were forced to use the longer northern pass.",
        "option_b": "The eastern trade route had been closed for three seasons; therefore, merchants were forced to use the longer northern pass.",
        "option_c": "The eastern trade route had been closed for three seasons therefore merchants were forced to use the longer northern pass.",
        "option_d": "The eastern trade route had been closed for three seasons, therefore merchants were forced to use the longer northern pass.",
        "correct_answer": "B",
        "explanation": "\"Therefore\" is a conjunctive adverb — a word like \"however,\" \"moreover,\" \"consequently,\" \"nevertheless,\" \"thus.\" Conjunctive adverbs cannot join independent clauses with a comma alone; that creates a comma splice. The correct punctuation: semicolon before the conjunctive adverb, comma after it (if the adverb is followed by a clause). Option A: comma + \"therefore\" + comma — the comma before \"therefore\" creates a comma splice; commas alone cannot join two independent clauses. Option C: no punctuation — a run-on sentence. Option D: comma + \"therefore\" with no comma after — this is the most dangerous wrong answer. The comma before a conjunctive adverb is a comma splice; additionally, \"therefore merchants\" without a comma after \"therefore\" is slightly awkward. Option B: semicolon before \"therefore\" (correctly joining the two independent clauses) and a comma after \"therefore\" (correctly setting off the conjunctive adverb from the rest of its clause). This is the standard pattern for all conjunctive adverbs: [independent clause]; [conjunctive adverb], [independent clause].",
        "success_narrative": "The King stares at the semicolon in option B as if he has never seen one before. \"A perfectly placed semicolon,\" he admits at last.",
        "failure_narrative": "You choose option D. The King accepts the comma splice. \"All my courtiers use it this way,\" he says.",
