        "question_stem": "Which version of the following sentence is grammatically correct?\n\nThe report detailing the failures of the king's three senior advisors ______ been suppressed for decades.",
        "option_a": "have",
        "option_b": "has",
        "option_c": "were",
        "option_d": "are",
        "correct_answer": "B",
        "explanation": "The subject of this sentence is \"The report\" — singular. The phrase \"detailing the failures of the king's three senior advisors\" is a participial phrase modifying \"report,\" not an additional subject. \"Advisors\" is the noun closest to the blank, which is why the plural verb is tempting — but \"advisors\" is the object of the preposition \"of,\" making it part of the modifying phrase, not the subject. The subject remains \"The report\" (singular), which requires a singular verb. Option B, \"has,\" is the singular present perfect form — correct. Option A, \"have,\" is plural. Option C, \"were,\" is past tense plural — both wrong tense and wrong number. Option D, \"are,\" is plural present. The ACT consistently tests subject-verb agreement across intervening phrases by placing plural nouns (often in prepositional phrases) between the true subject and its verb. The strategy: identify the true subject by asking \"what is this sentence about?\" — then check that the verb agrees with that noun, ignoring everything in between.",
        "success_narrative": "\"Has,\" you say, pointing to \"report.\" The King looks at \"advisors\" longingly. He wanted you to say \"have.\"",
        "failure_narrative": "You say \"have,\" drawn in by \"advisors.\" The King applauds. You have made the most common subject-verb agreement error on the ACT.",
