        "pre_narrative": "Two courtiers stand before the King. He gestures at them and says, \"When he told him that he needed to see him, he agreed immediately.\" No one in the room knows what he means. Including him. You must write it without ambiguity.",
        "passage": null,
        "question_stem": "Which version of the following sentence eliminates the ambiguous pronoun reference?\n\nWhen the general informed the duke that he would need to surrender the eastern garrison, he agreed without hesitation.",
        "option_a": "When the general informed the duke that he would need to surrender the eastern garrison, he agreed without hesitation.",
        "option_b": "When the general informed the duke that the duke would need to surrender the eastern garrison, the duke agreed without hesitation.",
        "option_c": "When the general informed the duke that he would need to surrender the eastern garrison, the general agreed without hesitation.",
        "option_d": "When the general, informing the duke that surrender was required, both agreed without hesitation.",
        "correct_answer": "B",
        "explanation": "The original sentence has two \"he\" pronouns, each of which could refer to either the general or the duke. \"He would need to surrender\" — who, the general or the duke? \"He agreed\" — who agreed? The sentence is genuinely ambiguous. Option A keeps both ambiguous pronouns unchanged. Option C replaces the second \"he\" with \"the general\" — so the general agreed (to the duke's surrender?), which is an odd meaning; and the first \"he\" still ambiguously refers to either party. Option D tries to restructure but creates a new error: \"both agreed\" implies both the general and the duke agreed, which introduces a new meaning not in the original. Option B, \"the duke would need to surrender... the duke agreed,\" eliminates ambiguity by naming the duke explicitly in both positions where the pronoun's referent was unclear. The principle: when a pronoun could logically refer to more than one noun in the sentence, replace the pronoun with the specific noun it refers to. On the ACT, ambiguous pronoun reference is a grammatical error, not a style preference.",
        "success_narrative": "\"The duke,\" you say. \"The duke would need to surrender. The duke agreed.\" The King sighs. \"The ambiguity was more interesting,\" he admits.",
