        "option_c": "The commander, having reviewed the battle plans before dawn, decided only to advance based on the initial reports.",
        "option_d": "Based only on the initial reports, having reviewed the battle plans before dawn, the commander made the decision to advance.",
        "correct_answer": "B",
        "explanation": "Option A contains a dangling modifier: \"Having reviewed the battle plans before dawn\" modifies the grammatical subject, which is \"the decision\" — but a decision cannot review battle plans. Additionally, \"based only on the initial reports\" is misplaced — it should modify how the decision was made (modify the verb), but its position after \"commander\" makes it ambiguously modify \"commander.\" Option B fixes the dangling modifier (the commander reviewed the plans, and the commander decided) and places \"based only on the initial reports\" directly after the verb \"decided\" — now it clearly modifies the decision-making process. The \"only\" is also correctly placed: \"based only on\" means the reports were the sole basis (modifying \"on\"). Option C moves \"only\" before \"to advance\" — this changes meaning: \"decided only to advance\" means the commander decided to advance and to do nothing else, rather than that the reports were the only basis. Option D stacks two opening modifiers, creating awkward double-modification and doesn't resolve the dangling participial. Option B is the only version that (1) attaches the participial phrase to the correct subject, (2) places \"based... on the initial reports\" to clearly modify the verb, and (3) positions \"only\" to modify \"on the initial reports\" rather than \"to advance.\"",
