        "explanation": "The sentence lists three infinitive actions required of officers. For parallel structure, all three items must follow the same grammatical pattern. The controlling verb is \"to [verb]\": \"to defend... to enforce... to serve.\" When items in a list follow a single \"to,\" they should all take the base verb form without repeating \"to.\" Option A: \"defend\" (base verb), \"the enforcement of its laws\" (noun phrase), \"serving its citizens\" (gerund phrase) — three different structures. Not parallel. Option B: \"to defend\" (infinitive), \"enforce\" (base verb), \"to serve\" (infinitive with repeated \"to\") — mixing whether \"to\" is repeated inconsistently. The ACT prefers either all with \"to\" or all without; option B also drops \"to\" before \"enforce\" but adds it before \"serve,\" creating inconsistency. Option D: \"defend\" (verb), \"its laws enforced\" (noun + past participle), \"serving citizens\" (gerund) — three different structures. Not parallel. Option C: \"defend... enforce... serve\" — all three are bare infinitive verb forms, all grammatically parallel under the opening \"to.\" This is the clean parallel structure the ACT requires.",
