        "correct_answer": "C",
        "explanation": "The subject of this sentence is \"Each\" — not \"captains.\" \"Each\" is an indefinite pronoun that is always grammatically singular, even when followed by a plural noun in a prepositional phrase. The phrase \"of the guard captains who patrol the northern wall\" is a modifier of \"Each,\" not an additional subject. The rule for \"each,\" \"every,\" \"either,\" \"neither,\" \"one,\" \"anyone,\" \"everyone,\" \"somebody,\" \"nobody\": all are singular and require singular verbs. Option C, \"is,\" is the singular present form — correct. Option A, \"are,\" is plural — attracted by \"captains\" but wrong because the subject is \"Each.\" Option B, \"were,\" is past tense plural — wrong in both number and tense. Option D, \"have been,\" is plural perfect tense — wrong in number. The sentence is in present tense (describing a standing requirement), which eliminates options B and D on tense grounds as well, but the core error is number: \"Each... is,\" not \"Each... are.\" Memorize the always-singular indefinite pronouns: each, every, either, neither, one, anyone, someone, everyone, no one, anybody, somebody, everybody, nobody.",
