        "explanation": "This question tests three apostrophe decisions: (1) \"Keep's\" — singular possessive, correctly formed as Keep's in all options (option C omits it — wrong); (2) \"whose\" vs. \"who's\" — \"who's\" is a contraction of \"who is\" or \"who has.\" The sentence needs the possessive pronoun \"whose\" (whose design = the design belonging to it). \"Who's design dates to the founding era\" would mean \"who is design dates,\" which is nonsense. So \"whose\" is correct; (3) \"its\" vs. \"it's\" — \"it's\" is a contraction of \"it is\" or \"it has.\" The sentence needs the possessive pronoun \"its\" (its original colors = the colors belonging to it). \"It has it is original colors\" makes no sense. So \"its\" is correct. Option B: \"Keep's\" ✓, \"whose\" ✓, \"its\" ✓ — all three correct. Option A: \"who's\" ✗ (should be \"whose\"), \"it's\" ✗ (should be \"its\"). Option C: \"Keeps\" ✗ (missing apostrophe for possessive), \"it's\" ✗. Option D: \"who's\" ✗ (should be \"whose\"), \"its\" ✓ — two out of three. The memory rule: possessive pronouns never take apostrophes (its, whose, hers, theirs, ours); apostrophes in these words always signal contractions (it's = it is; who's = who is).",
